1
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:06,580
This is Chillax. Chill,

2
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:10,020
plus relax. It's a terminal first manager for

3
00:00:10,030 --> 00:00:11,620
an AI product team.

4
00:00:11,630 --> 00:00:13,540
You hire agent employees,

5
00:00:13,550 --> 00:00:15,380
each backed by a coding agent,

6
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:18,740
like Claude Code. You talk to a project manager,

7
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:21,700
and you stay where a real stakeholder stays,

8
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:24,340
approving sprints and releases while the team

9
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:27,520
does the work. Let's build something real.

10
00:00:28,677 --> 00:00:31,627
We'll start from a plan already written with Claude,

11
00:00:31,637 --> 00:00:33,787
a LinkedIn recommendation app.

12
00:00:33,797 --> 00:00:36,987
In any Git folder, type Chillax.

13
00:00:36,997 --> 00:00:39,147
The first run sets everything up.

14
00:00:39,157 --> 00:00:40,507
You get a dashboard,

15
00:00:40,517 --> 00:00:43,787
a team view, chat, a Kanban board,

16
00:00:43,797 --> 00:00:46,027
a live feed of agent activity,

17
00:00:46,037 --> 00:00:48,907
dev, QA, and production environments,

18
00:00:48,917 --> 00:00:51,067
an inbox, and usage.

19
00:00:51,077 --> 00:00:53,707
Only one person is on the team so far,

20
00:00:53,717 --> 00:00:56,107
a project manager, hired for you.

21
00:00:56,117 --> 00:00:59,147
In Settings, you choose the default coding agent

22
00:00:59,157 --> 00:01:01,447
and model for every new hire.

23
00:01:02,521 --> 00:01:04,751
Now, the stakeholder meeting.

24
00:01:04,761 --> 00:01:08,191
Open chat with the PM and point it at the plan.

25
00:01:08,201 --> 00:01:11,391
It reads the plan, creates epics and stories with

26
00:01:11,401 --> 00:01:12,751
acceptance criteria,

27
00:01:12,761 --> 00:01:14,991
and groups them into a sprint.

28
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:17,551
It also hires the people it needs:

29
00:01:17,561 --> 00:01:19,311
a tech lead, developers,

30
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:23,151
a QA engineer. The PM runs the team.

31
00:01:23,161 --> 00:01:24,971
You run the vision.

32
00:01:26,125 --> 00:01:28,515
The first gate lands in your inbox.

33
00:01:28,525 --> 00:01:30,195
Approve the sprint.

34
00:01:30,205 --> 00:01:33,475
Press A or click. Until you do,

35
00:01:33,485 --> 00:01:35,555
no agent touches the code.

36
00:01:35,565 --> 00:01:38,195
Once you approve, the developers pick up their

37
00:01:38,205 --> 00:01:40,575
assigned tickets on their own.

38
00:01:41,632 --> 00:01:44,822
Here's the loop. Every ticket gets its own Git

39
00:01:44,832 --> 00:01:46,182
worktree and branch,

40
00:01:46,192 --> 00:01:48,102
so agents never collide,

41
00:01:48,112 --> 00:01:50,502
and your checkout stays untouched.

42
00:01:50,512 --> 00:01:51,942
A developer builds,

43
00:01:51,952 --> 00:01:53,702
Chillax runs the tests,

44
00:01:53,712 --> 00:01:55,862
and the tech lead reviews the diff.

45
00:01:55,872 --> 00:01:58,342
Approved work merges into dev.

46
00:01:58,352 --> 00:02:01,862
Then, QA checks every acceptance criterion.

47
00:02:01,872 --> 00:02:04,822
On the board, you watch each ticket move.

48
00:02:04,832 --> 00:02:06,822
In progress, review,

49
00:02:06,832 --> 00:02:10,822
QA, done. Every comment and decision is kept on

50
00:02:10,832 --> 00:02:12,002
the ticket.

51
00:02:13,072 --> 00:02:16,422
Watch it your way. The Live tab streams every

52
00:02:16,432 --> 00:02:19,782
tool call. The Team tab shows each employee in

53
00:02:19,792 --> 00:02:22,262
a grid, and you can chat with any of them for

54
00:02:22,272 --> 00:02:26,102
status. The usage tab breaks down tokens and cost,

55
00:02:26,112 --> 00:02:28,342
per ticket and per employee.

56
00:02:28,352 --> 00:02:32,582
The same views are in your browser on port 4747.

57
00:02:32,592 --> 00:02:35,302
And when an agent runs an app in its worktree,

58
00:02:35,312 --> 00:02:36,902
it appears on the dashboard,

59
00:02:36,912 --> 00:02:38,822
so you see real progress,

60
00:02:38,832 --> 00:02:40,402
not just reports.

61
00:02:41,561 --> 00:02:44,111
Not every job needs the same model.

62
00:02:44,121 --> 00:02:47,231
For any hire, you can swap the coding agent,

63
00:02:47,241 --> 00:02:49,471
the model, and the effort.

64
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:51,711
A lighter model for QA,

65
00:02:51,721 --> 00:02:54,091
the strongest one for development.

66
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:57,310
Plans change mid-sprint.

67
00:02:57,320 --> 00:02:59,150
File a request anytime,

68
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:01,950
and it becomes a work item for the right person.

69
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:04,990
Mark it high priority and it goes to the front.

70
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:06,910
Agents flag things too.

71
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:10,510
If one is stuck, say it needs a real API key.

72
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,430
It lands in your inbox.

73
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:15,150
Answer it or chat with the agent directly.

74
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:17,950
And when the tech lead finds problems in review,

75
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:21,150
it suggests new stories and who should take them.

76
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:24,010
The PM creates and assigns them.

77
00:03:25,086 --> 00:03:28,516
Need to stop? Pause freezes new work.

78
00:03:28,526 --> 00:03:30,676
Quit, and nothing is lost,

79
00:03:30,686 --> 00:03:33,956
because every state change is an event in a log.

80
00:03:33,966 --> 00:03:36,996
Come back and Chillax rebuilds the team,

81
00:03:37,006 --> 00:03:40,436
chats, tickets, and inbox exactly where you left

82
00:03:40,446 --> 00:03:44,096
them. Resume, and the agents pick up the work.

83
00:03:45,164 --> 00:03:46,514
When the sprint is done,

84
00:03:46,524 --> 00:03:48,274
the second gate appears.

85
00:03:48,284 --> 00:03:52,194
Release approval. Environments shows QA,

86
00:03:52,204 --> 00:03:55,154
ready to promote. Approve the version and its

87
00:03:55,164 --> 00:03:59,074
notes, and Chillax merges QA into main and tags

88
00:03:59,084 --> 00:04:02,274
it. Nothing reaches production without you.

89
00:04:02,284 --> 00:04:05,074
Then, the dashboard invites you to plan the next

90
00:04:05,084 --> 00:04:06,014
sprint.

91
00:04:07,174 --> 00:04:08,844
Here's a second project.

92
00:04:08,854 --> 00:04:11,564
I gave the PM about two lines.

93
00:04:11,574 --> 00:04:15,164
A directory of free alternatives to paid software.

94
00:04:15,174 --> 00:04:17,724
Sprint by sprint, the team built,

95
00:04:17,734 --> 00:04:20,444
reviewed, tested, and released it.

96
00:04:20,454 --> 00:04:22,204
This is the live site.

97
00:04:22,214 --> 00:04:24,924
It isn't perfect, and the next improvement is

98
00:04:24,934 --> 00:04:26,424
just another sprint.

99
00:04:27,565 --> 00:04:29,235
An honest trade-off.

100
00:04:29,245 --> 00:04:30,755
This plans heavily,

101
00:04:30,765 --> 00:04:33,635
so it costs more tokens and time than handing

102
00:04:33,645 --> 00:04:35,715
one prompt to one agent.

103
00:04:35,725 --> 00:04:38,035
In return, you get structure.

104
00:04:38,045 --> 00:04:40,115
Every piece of work has a ticket,

105
00:04:40,125 --> 00:04:42,195
with the reasoning in its comments.

106
00:04:42,205 --> 00:04:43,875
That's what makes it manageable,

107
00:04:43,885 --> 00:04:45,955
like an actual organization.

108
00:04:45,965 --> 00:04:48,915
Chillax, you are the stakeholder.

109
00:04:48,925 --> 00:04:50,995
Chillax runs the company.

110
00:04:51,005 --> 00:04:55,315
Follow along at github.com slash prayushkale slash

111
00:04:55,325 --> 00:04:57,205
chillax dash app.
